OpenAI today said it added <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/icloud/">iCloud</a> Mail and Calendar plugins to ChatGPT.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/06/General-macOS-Mail-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2250" height="1266" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-900439" /><br/>
With the &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Calendar integration, ChatGPT can read, schedule, and update events that show up on your iPhone's Calendar app or any third-party app that uses &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Calendar. The Mail integration lets ChatGPT search and read &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Mail, manage messages and drafts, send email, and look up &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; contacts.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Mail uses an Apple-provided email address that ends in @icloud.com, @me.com, or @mac.com. ChatGPT can only see your &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Mail, not other accounts that are in the iOS Mail app, like Gmail or Outlook. You will need to create an app-specific password for both &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Mail and &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; Calendar.<br/>
<br/>
&zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; plugins give ChatGPT some of the same access that <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI has to personal data, expanding what the chatbot can do. &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; access can also be used for Dots, the proactive AI agent that OpenAI launched last month.<br/>
<br/>
